; The census questions, re-asked as derivations (extension §2), so that
; `modality-cross-check.sh` can hold the rules engine and `writ check` up against
; each other on island.claims's four properties.
;
; POLARITY. All four are `possible`, which is the one modality with a POSITIVE
; encoding: the relation is the set of reachable situations satisfying F, and
; NON-EMPTY means the property holds. (The `live`/`never` encodings are the
; opposite — counterexample sets, empty when the property holds. See
; river.rules, which has one of each, for why.)
;
; Two of these four are expected to derive NOTHING, and an empty answer set is
; the answer, not a failure: cal said "I am a knave", which no kind makes
; consistent, so the move that would read him is the declared gap.

(load "ct.rules")

; Every native classified at once. Empty ⟺ `fails census-completable`.
(satisfies census-completable
  (and (defined abe.kind) (defined bea.kind) (defined cal.kind)))

; "Which natives could be knights?", asked once per native — the readings under
; which each is a knight. Non-empty ⟺ that native can be one.
(satisfies abe-can-be-knight (is abe.kind knight))
(satisfies bea-can-be-knight (is bea.kind knight))
(satisfies cal-can-be-knight (is cal.kind knight))
